/*
# Everything is an object
At compile time, all constructs are a javascript object, literally
A file is an object of objects // so we don't have to specify module structure
A folder is an object of files (objects) // modules are just nested folders
A type is an object with certain structure // like in zig
A number is an object with certain structure // like in zig
A string!? is an object with this structure: {len: u32, ptr: u32}


# Strings
There's two different types of strings in this language:
`string` and `[]u8`

## `string`
`string` is strings with heavy javascript envolvement
theres two variants to create `string`
1. "" - an inline variant
  - works like "``" in Javascript
    but instead of using `${}`, the syntax is `{}`
    (like in rust, except as powerful as in javascript)
2. \\\ - a multiline variant
  - core ideas are similar, but instead of "\n", you literally create a new line
    you can limit a line to create comment, or trailing spaces:
    \\\Theres a line       \\\ with trailing spaces

## `[]u8`
`[]u8` is just like a `[]const u8` in zig, with the difference of bytes being mutable
this is very straightforward, you just create a string with single quotes:
> `example: []u8 = 'This is an array of bytes and nothing more'`
you can create just a char by just having a single character (non utf8!) in the quotes
> `example2: u8 = 'a'`


# Constness and mutability
In this language there is no `const`, or `mut` keywords/identifiers
Every piece of data  can be modified
You can also reassign constant stuff, like types, with your own stuff
You can modify anything because at compiletime everything is just a JS object (think data of a type)
You can easily extend even types like `string` to have more data just like that:
`string.more_data: []u8` // there! now all of your `""` strings are invalid!
`string.more_data :: []` and now it is valid again! (comptime assignment of value to a type parameter is equivalent to setting default value, := will error because types may not have data)


# In-depth on type system

## Example
We will examine the type system using `str` and `Option` example:
```
Option :: (T: type) => #freeze union(enum) { none some: T }
foo :: struct { bar: []u8 }
```

## Data model
Every global identifier is a javascript object.
In the cases above:
- `str` contains something like that: // lsp look: `str           type`
    `str = { // type info
        type: "type",
        name: "str",
        struct: { fields: [{ name: "data", type: "[]u8" }] }
    }` // which is actually very similar to how zig does types :)
- `Option` contains something like that: // lsp look: `Option(T)             type`
    `Option = { // type info
        type: "function",
        show_name: "Option(T)",
        show_type: "type",
        comptime: true,
        params: [{ name: "T", type: "type" }], // type[]
        body: /* generated js function that generates necessary types (oh god, I just understood the scope of this thing… ) */,
        methods: {} // Stores attached methods like `is_some`
    }`

## Type mutability / Transactional Type Modifications
You may mutate types, freeze them and clone them

## Mutation
If type is not frozen, you are free to change it hovewer you please:
```
foo.bar: []s16 // no errors, changed in global scope, take notice that this bumps minor version of this type
```

*/
